rmula: see text], and for the homology representation of [Formula: see text]. We also study the cut complex of the squared path and the grid graph. Our techniques include tools from combinatorial topology, discrete Morse theory, and equivariant poset topology.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (4)\n\n## References (17)\n\n... Cut complexes of graphs sit at the interface of graph theory, algebraic combinatorics, and topological combinatorics. They are close in spirit to independence complexes, clique complexes, and Alexander-dual constructions appearing in the study of squarefree monomial ideals; see, for example, the graph-theoretic ideals in [1,2] and the later development of cut complexes in [3,4,5]. This paper resolves a recurrence conjecture from [ Thus every facet of ∆ k (G) has cardinality n − k whenever a disconnected k-set exists. ...\n\n... Bayer et al. proved that, for k ≥ 2 and n ≥ k + 3, ∆ k (P 2 n ) is shellable and homotopy equivalent to a wedge of spheres of dimension n − k − 1 [5,Theorem 4.4]. Hence its reduced homology is concentrated in top degree. ...\n\n... The conjecture in [5] states that, for fixed r = n − k ≥ 3, the diagonal sequence β(k, k + r) has vanishing rth finite difference. Written in the notation above, the conjectured identity is ...\n\nBetti Numbers of Cut Complexes of Squared Paths and a Recurrence Conjecture\n\nPreprint\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * May 2026\n\n  * Yutong Zhang\n  * Yaoran Yang\n\nFor a graph G on [n], the k-cut complex $Δ_k(G)$ has facets $[n]\\setminus T$, where T ranges over the disconnected k-vertex induced subgraphs of G. Bayer, Denker, Jelić Milutinović, Sundaram, and Xue proved that the k-cut complex of the squared path $P_n^2$ is shellable for $n\\ge k+3$ and conjectured a finite-difference recurrence for its top reduced Betti number along every diagonal $n-k=r$. We prove the recurrence by giving the exact formula $β(k,n)=\\binom{n-1}{k-1}-\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min\\{k-1,n-k\\}}\\binom{k-1}{j}(n-k-j+1)+(n-k)$ for $r=n-k\\ge3$. Equivalently, for fixed $r\\ge3$, the diagonal sequence $B_r(k)=β(k,k+r)$ is a polynomial in k of degree $r-1$, and therefore $\\nabla^rB_r(k)=0$. The proof uses a complementary-face enumeration: among complements with size at least k, all bad complements have size k or k+1, and they are, respectively, connected k-subsets of $P_n^2$ and intervals of length k+1. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopology of Cut Complexes II (https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.08158)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Title: Topology of Cut Complexes II ... We continue the study of the $k$-cut complex $\\Delta_k(G)$ of a graph $G$ initiated in the paper of Bayer, Denker, Jeli\\'c Milutinovi\\'c, Rowlands, Sundaram and Xue [Topology of cut complexes of graphs, SIAM J. on Discrete Math.\nTitle: Topology of Cut Complexes II\nAuthors: Margaret Bayer, Mark Denker, Marija Jelić Milutinović, Sheila Sundaram, Lei Xue\nDate: Thu Jul 11 03:25:38 2024\n\nWe continue the study of the $k$-cut complex $\\Delta_k(G)$ of a graph $G$ initiated in the paper of Bayer, Denker, Jeli\\'c Milutinovi\\'c, Rowlands, Sundaram and Xue [Topology of cut complexes of graphs, SIAM J. on Discrete Math. 38(2): 1630--1675 (2024)]. We give explicit formulas for the $f$- and $h$-polynomials of the cut complex $\\Delta_k(G_1+G_2) $ of the disjoint union of two graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$, and for the homology representation of $\\Delta_k(K_m+K_n)$. We also study the cut complex of the squared path and the grid graph. Our techniques include tools from combinatorial topology, discrete Morse theory and equivariant poset topology.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopology of Cut Complexes II | eNauka (https://enauka.gov.rs/handle/123